@Bob
Not knowing the tennis player, I started Cookaburras with a C -- oops.

After looking all over, and even making sure all the O's were not zeros [they look alike in my app's font] I went and read the column.  Problem solved!

@Elbridge Gerry

Except it is. If you know your science, genus and class are several levels apart. Yes, I get the wordplay, and I understand that you can find the two words together in dictionaries. Certainly, the clue falls within the letter of the law, but still seems dodgy to me. 

It’s like making up a clue that implies that a minute and a week have equivalency.

@Jim

I did too.  To me, that's not the barometer of a good clue.

And maybe there's a fine line here, but I'm talking about good clues, not clues that are technically fair.

@Petrol

Right? I was also surprised by the second L in WOOLLY. 

As for MAMMOTH (the Polish mamut), one of the Polish major dictionaries gives a surprisingly inaccurate etymology: "z jakiegoś języka języka syberyjskiego" ("from some Syberian language"). "Some"? Well, ok...

@klh
I don't think that list does go on. I think that's the complete list.
